<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Clontarf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1014">1014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Clontarf was fought on 23 April 1014 north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. The army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Bóruma</a>, high king of Ireland, defeated the king of Leinster, the Norse of Dublin and their allies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and the Isles, but Brian himself was killed. It was later remembered as the victory that broke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> power in Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf.jpg" alt="Battle of Clontarf" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hugh Frazer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Battle_of_Clontarf%27,_oil_on_canvas_painting_by_Hugh_Frazer,_1826.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> had held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> since 838, and by the tenth century the Kingdom of Dublin controlled wide territories around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>. Brian Boru of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-cais">Dál gCais</a> rose from the kingship of Munster to become undisputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a> by 1002, displacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-domnaill">Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>. In 999 he and Máel Sechnaill had defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitric-silkbeard">Sigtrygg Silkbeard</a>, King of Dublin, and Máel Mórda mac Murchada, King of Leinster, at the Battle of Glenmama.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and Leinster raided Meath in 1013, Brian camped outside Dublin from September to the end of the year. Sigtrygg sought help overseas from Sigurd Hlodvirsson, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, and Brodir of the Isle of Man; Njáls saga says he promised each of them the kingship of Ireland. Their fleets reached Dublin in Holy Week 1014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Contemporary sources give no order of battle. According to the Cogad Gáedel re Gallaib, the foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> formed the front line, with the men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and then the Leinstermen behind, while Sigtrygg watched from the walls of Dublin. Brian, in his seventies, stayed behind to pray. The fighting lasted from first light all day. When the Dublin and Leinster forces broke, the incoming tide cut them off from a wood and carried away their ships, and many drowned. Brian's grandson Toirdelbach drowned in the pursuit, his son Murchad killed Sigurd and then fell, and Brodir found Brian in his tent and killed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Between 7,000 and 10,000 men are estimated to have died. Brian was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>, and Máel Sechnaill was restored as High King until his death in 1022. The loss of both Brian and his heir Murchad opened a succession struggle among his sons Donnchad and Tadc. Dublin was reduced to a lesser power, and in 1052 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-mael-na-mbo">Diarmait mac Máel na mBó</a>, King of Leinster, captured it. Historians have long debated the battle: the popular view sees a war of Irish against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, while revisionists see an Irish civil war with Vikings on both sides.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Clontarf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After defeat at <b>Clontarf</b> in 1014, Dublin was dominated by Irish overkings.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dublin">Kingdom of Dublin</a></li><li><blockquote>Tadhg Mór Ua Cellaig fell fighting for Brian Bóruma at <b>Clontarf</b> in 1014.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ui-maine">Kingdom of Ui Maine</a></li></ul></section>
